<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eleusinian Mysteries</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Eleusinian Mysteries were the secret initiation rites of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demeter">Demeter</a> and her daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persephone">Persephone</a>, or Kore, held each year at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleusis">Eleusis</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a> under the supervision of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. Initiates walked in procession from Athens along the Sacred Way and were promised a better fate after death. The rites drew people from across the Greek and Roman world until the sanctuary was abandoned at the end of the fourth century CE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eleusinian-mysteries.jpg" alt="Eleusinian Mysteries" width="320" height="384" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marsyas · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NAMA_Myst%C3%A8res_d%27Eleusis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
